  "text_md": "- α1,1,p(r,ϕ,S) ≡\n![](<2503.07537_pg4_images/imageFile18.png>)\n- aε1,2p(r,ϕ,S) ≡\n2 aεi,2p(r,ϕ,S), i ∈ {1,2}, αi,j(r,ϕ,S,t) ≡ t−\n![](<2503.07537_pg4_images/imageFile14.png>)\n![](<2503.07537_pg4_images/imageFile23.png>)\n![](<2503.07537_pg4_images/imageFile1.png>)\nai(r,ϕ,S,t) ≡ t−n4 ai,n(r,ϕ,S) + t−\nt kr2 + 1\n2π T(r)\n(Q(S)X1(ϕ,r) + P(S)X2(ϕ,r)) ,\nr ν(r)\nIf G(x,y,S) ≡ 0 and ε = 0, then r(t) ≡ ̺0 and ϕ(t) ≡ ν(̺0)t + φ0 with arbitrary constants ̺0 and φ0. Numerical analysis of the system with G(x,y,S)  ≡ 0 and ε = 0 shows that, depending on the values of the perturbation parameters, two cases are possible. In the ﬁrst case, the amplitude of the solutions either tends to zero or grows until it reaches the boundary of the considered domain, depending on the sign of Q0 (see Fig. 1, a). In the second case, the solutions with an asymptotically constant amplitude r(t) ≈ r0 may appear (see Fig. 1, b). The emergence of such solutions is associated with the capture of the system into resonance. If ε = 0, the stochastic perturbations may disrupt such a behaviour (see Fig. 1, c).\nX1(ϕ,r) ≡ xˆ1,0\nwhere sn(u,k), cn(u,k), dn(u,k) are the Jacoby elliptic functions, K(k) is the complete elliptic integral of the ﬁrst kind, and kr ∈ (0,1) is a root of the equation (kr + kr−1)−2 = ϑr2/2. Deﬁne auxiliary 2πperiodic functions\nNote that the level lines {(x1,x2) ∈ R2 : 2U(x1) + x22 = r2} for all 0 < |r| < (2ϑ)−1/2 correspond to T(r)-periodic solutions\nT(r) ≡ 4K(kr) kr2 + 1, ν(r) ≡\n![](<2503.07537_pg4_images/imageFile13.png>)\n,\n![](<2503.07537_pg4_images/imageFile7.png>)\n,\n4 O.A. SULTANOV\n![](<2503.07537_pg4_images/imageFile9.png>)\n- xˆ2,0(t,r) ≡ r cn\nwhere\n∂r\na2,n(r,ϕ,S) ≡ −\n(Q(S)X1(ϕ,r) + P(S)X2(ϕ,r)) ,\n![](<2503.07537_pg4_images/imageFile19.png>)\n,kr dn\n![](<2503.07537_pg4_images/imageFile2.png>)\n- xˆ1,0(t,r) ≡ r sn\n![](<2503.07537_pg4_images/imageFile21.png>)\n![](<2503.07537_pg4_images/imageFile6.png>)\nHence, the transformation (9) is invertible for all 0 < |r| < (2ϑ)−1/2 and ϕ ∈ [0,2π). Then, by applying Itˆo’s formula [39, §4.2], it can be shown that in the variables (r,ϕ) system (7) takes the form (1) with µ(t) ≡ t−1/4, s0 = 3/2, si = 0,\n- aε2,2p(r,ϕ,S) ≡ −\n![](<2503.07537_pg4_images/imageFile22.png>)\n![](<2503.07537_pg4_images/imageFile3.png>)",
